== Governor of New Jersey ==
===2025 election===
{{Main|2025 New Jersey gubernatorial election}}

[[File:2025 New Jersey gubernatorial election results map by county.svg|250px|thumb|right|County results map of the 2025 New Jersey gubernatorial election. Sherrill counties in blue; Ciattarelli in red.]]
Sherrill launched her campaign for governor on November 18, 2024, seeking to succeed term-limited incumbent [[Phil Murphy]].<ref>Manchester, Julia (November 18, 2024). [https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/4995682-nj-rep-mikie-sherrill-launches-governor-campaign/ Rep. Mikie Sherrill launches bid for New Jersey governor] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241123062046/https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/4995682-nj-rep-mikie-sherrill-launches-governor-campaign/ |date=November 23, 2024 }}. ''[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]''. Retrieved August 3, 2025.</ref> Her campaign emphasized affordability (particularly property taxes and utility costs), protecting children, and increasing government accountability.<ref>{{cite news |title=New Jersey election 2025: What to know about Mikie Sherrill and her priorities |url=https://whyy.org/articles/nj-election-2025-mikie-sherrill-governor-priorities |work=WHYY |date=October 29, 2025 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref> She was endorsed by organizations including the New Jersey Education Association, [[Sierra Club]], [[Health Professionals and Allied Employees]], and several newspapers owned by Gannett.<ref>{{cite web |title=Endorsed Candidates |url=https://election.njea.org/endorsements2025 |website=New Jersey Education Association |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=The Sierra Club Endorses Mikie Sherrill for NJ Governor |url=https://www.sierraclub.org/new-jersey/sierra-club-endorses-mikie-sherrill-nj-governor |website=Sierra Club New Jersey Chapter |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Gannett newspapers endorse Mikie Sherrill |url=https://newjerseyglobe.com/governor/gannett-newspapers-endorse-mikie-sherill |work=New Jersey Globe |date=October 24, 2025 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref>

In the June 10, 2025, Democratic primary, Sherrill won the nomination with approximately 34% of the vote in a crowded field that included Newark Mayor [[Ras Baraka]], Jersey City Mayor [[Steven Fulop]], and former state Senate President [[Stephen Sweeney|Steve Sweeney]].<ref>{{cite news |title=It's Sherrill vs. Ciattarelli for New Jersey governor. Experts weigh in on how each candidate wins the race |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/philadelphia/news/sherrill-ciattarelli-new-jersey-governor-race/ |work=CBS News Philadelphia |date=June 11, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250613093645/https://www.cbsnews.com/philadelphia/news/sherrill-ciattarelli-new-jersey-governor-race/ |archive-date=June 13, 2025 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref> She faced Republican nominee [[Jack Ciattarelli]], a former state assemblyman who had narrowly lost the 2021 gubernatorial election to Murphy, in the November 4 general election.<ref>{{cite web |title=2025 New Jersey gubernatorial election |url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_New_Jersey_gubernatorial_election |website=Wikipedia |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref>

The general-election campaign focused heavily on cost-of-living issues, with both candidates pledging to address high property taxes and utility rates.<ref>{{cite news |title=NJ governor's race: Where Sherrill, Ciattarelli stand on key issues |url=https://www.njspotlightnews.org/2025/10/nj-governors-race-where-sherrill-ciattarelli-stand-on-key-issues |work=NJ Spotlight News |date=October 31, 2025 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref> Late polling showed a competitive race, with some surveys indicating a close contest,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://emersoncollegepolling.com/new-york-city-mayor-and-new-jersey-governor-2025/ |title=New York City Mayor and New Jersey Governor 2025 |date=October 30, 2025 |website=Emerson College Polling |archive-date=October 31, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251031014129/https://emersoncollegepolling.com/new-york-city-mayor-and-new-jersey-governor-2025/ |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref> but Sherrill won decisively with 57% of the vote to Ciattarelli's 43%, performing strongly in suburban counties and flipping some voters who had supported Donald Trump in the 2024 presidential election.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2025/11/04/us/elections/results-new-jersey-governor.html |title=New Jersey Governor Election Results 2025 |website=The New York Times |date=November 6, 2025 |archive-date=November 5, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251105170227/https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2025/11/04/us/elections/results-new-jersey-governor.html |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Cohn |first=Nate |title=Two Signs That Democrats Flipped Trump Supporters on Tuesday |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/11/06/upshot/election-turnout-trump-hispanics.html |work=The New York Times |date=November 6, 2025 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Cohn |first=Nate |title=11 Data Points and Discoveries That Stuck With Us in 2025 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2025/12/30/upshot/year-in-review.html |work=The New York Times |date=December 30, 2025 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref> Her victory made Sherrill the second woman to serve as governor of New Jersey (after Republican [[Christine Todd Whitman]]), the first Democratic woman in the role, and the first female military veteran to serve as governor of any U.S. state.<ref name="auto" /><ref name="auto1" />

===Tenure===
Sherrill was sworn in as New Jersey's 57th governor on January 20, 2026, at the [[New Jersey Performing Arts Center]] in [[Newark, New Jersey|Newark]], the first gubernatorial inauguration held outside the state capital of [[Trenton, New Jersey|Trenton]] since the tenure of [[William Livingston]], New Jersey's first governor.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://newjerseyglobe.com/governor/sherrill-inauguration-will-be-in-newark-not-trenton/ |title=Sherrill inauguration will be in Newark, not Trenton |last=Wildstein |first=David |date=December 17, 2025 |work=New Jersey Globe |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Johnson |first=Brent |title=Mikie Sherrill sworn in as N.J. governor as new era begins |url=https://www.nj.com/politics/2026/01/mikie-sherrill-sworn-in-as-nj-governor-as-new-era-begins.html |work=NJ.com153 |date=January 20, 2026 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref> In her inaugural address, she emphasized affordability and government efficiency and took a defiant tone toward national political divisions.<ref name="NBC-2026-01-20">{{cite news |title=Mikie Sherrill sworn in as New Jersey governor with defiant tone toward Trump |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/politics-news/democrat-mikie-sherrill-sworn-new-jersey-governor-defiant-tone-trump-rcna255101 |work=NBC News |agency=Associated Press |date=January 20, 2026 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref> The inaugural ball was held at the [[American Dream (shopping mall)|American Dream Mall]] in [[East Rutherford, New Jersey|East Rutherford]], featuring performances by [[Naughty by Nature]] and other New Jersey artists.<ref>{{cite news |last=Wallace |first=Amanda |title=Of course, NJ's new governor holds inaugural ball in a mall |url=https://www.northjersey.com/story/news/new-jersey/2026/01/20/mikie-sherrill-nj-governor-inaugural-ball-2026-american-dream-mall/88271500007/ |work=NorthJersey.com |date=January 20, 2026 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref> On her first day in office, Sherrill signed two executive orders: one declaring a state of emergency on utility costs and imposing a temporary freeze on electric rate increases, and one aimed at expanding clean energy sources.<ref name="NBC-2026-01-20" /><ref>{{cite web |title=Promise Kept: Governor Sherrill Takes Bold Action with Executive Orders Declaring State of Emergency on Utility Costs |url=https://www.nj.gov/governor/news/2026/20260120a.shtml |website=NJ.gov |date=January 20, 2026 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref> Due to severe winter weather, Sherrill declared a statewide state of emergency on January 23 to facilitate response efforts and resource allocation.<ref>{{cite web |title=Governor Sherrill Declares State of Emergency Due to Severe Winter Storm |url=https://www.nj.gov/governor/news/2026/20260123b.shtml |website=NJ.gov |date=January 23, 2026 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref> As of late January 2026, Sherrill's early tenure has focused on fulfilling campaign pledges related to affordability amid ongoing economic pressures.<ref>{{cite news |title='No bulls--t about her': Mikie Sherrill will quickly be put to the test as New Jersey governor |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/01/19/no-bulls-t-about-her-mikie-sherrill-will-quickly-be-put-to-the-test-as-new-jersey-governor-00735007 |work=Politico |last=Fernandez |first=Madison |date=January 19, 2026 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref>

In February, Sherrill issued an executive order barring [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] (ICE) officials from operating on state-owned property, which includes office buildings, parking lots, and parkways.<ref>Blackburn, Zach (February 11, 2026). [https://newjerseyglobe.com/immigration/sherrill-signs-order-barring-ice-from-mounting-civil-operations-from-state-property/ Sherrill signs order barring ICE from mounting civil operations from state property]. ''New Jersey Globe''. Retrieved February 24, 2026.</ref> The [[United States Department of Justice]] subsequently sued New Jersey, arguing that Sherrill had violated the [[Supremacy Clause]].<ref>Tulley, Tracy; Ferré-Sadurní, Luis (February 24, 2026). [https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/24/nyregion/doj-sues-new-jersey-ice-sherrill.html Trump's Justice Department Sues New Jersey Governor Over ICE Enforcement]. ''[[The New York Times]]''. Retrieved February 24, 2026.</ref>

On March 10, 2026, Sherrill announced her Fiscal Year 2027 budget proposal, totaling a record $60.7 billion focused on affordability, fiscal responsibility, and education, aiming to cut costs amid New Jersey's structural deficit. The proposal includes $2 billion in proposed expenditure reductions, $4.2 billion in property tax relief, $22.5 billion for school aid, and $33 million for youth mental health services, while aiming to close corporate tax loopholes.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Governor Sherrill Presents Fiscal Year 2027 Budget |url=https://www.nj.gov/governor/news/2026/20260310b.shtml |access-date=2026-04-12 |website=Governor Mikie Sherrill |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Aguas |first1=Tonanziht |last2=Ambrose |first2=Alex |last3=Chen |first3=Peter |last4=Holom-Trundy |first4=Brittany |last5=Ubel |first5=Marleina |date=2026-03-11 |title=How Gov. Sherrill's Fiscal Year 2027 Budget Measures Up |url=https://www.njpp.org/publications/report/how-gov-sherrills-fiscal-year-2027-budget-measures-up/ |access-date=2026-04-12 |website=New Jersey Policy Perspective |language=en-US}}</ref> On April 8, Sherrill signed legislation lifting a state nuclear moratorium in front of the [[Hope Creek Nuclear Generating Station]], aiming to expand access to clean energy. Sherrill also announced the launch of the state's Nuclear Task Force to seek out new nuclear energy projects.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Governor Sherrill Signs Legislation Lifting 50 Year Nuclear Moratorium, Launches Nuclear Task Force at Salem Nuclear Power Plant |url=https://www.nj.gov/governor/news/2026/20260408a.shtml |access-date=2026-04-12 |website=Governor Mikie Sherrill |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Schneider |first=Aliya |date=2026-04-09 |title=New Jersey Eases Rules to Spur New Nuclear Development |url=https://www.governing.com/infrastructure/new-jersey-eases-rules-to-spur-new-nuclear-development |access-date=2026-04-12 |website=Governing |language=en}}</ref>

In early September 2026, an investigation began into Lieutenant Governor [[Dale Caldwell]]'s "alleged inappropriate behavior with women and possible ethics violations that include the purported offer of state jobs". Sherrill appointed Attorney General [[Christopher Porrino]] to lead the investigation, which, on September 24, ended with the conclusion that the allegations against Caldwell were corroborated. On the same day, Sherrill called for Caldwell's resignation; Caldwell announced his resignation on September 25.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://newjerseyglobe.com/governor/sherrill-caldwell-report/| title= Sherrill gives Caldwell until tomorrow to resign after report substantiates sexual harassment, ethics violations| first= David| last= Wildstein| work= [[New Jersey Globe]]| date= September 24, 2026| access-date= September 24, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= Wildstein| first= David| date= 2026-09-25| title= Caldwell will resign as lieutenant governor after harassment, ethics findings| url= https://newjerseyglobe.com/section-2/caldwell-will-resign-as-lieutenant-governor-after-harassment-ethics-findings/| access-date= 2026-09-25| website= New Jersey Globe| language= en-US}}</ref><ref name= ":02">{{Cite web |last= Wildstein| first= David| date= 2026-09-04| title= Lt. Governor Dale Caldwell under investigation after allegations of inappropriate behavior| url= https://newjerseyglobe.com/governor/lt-governor-dale-caldwell-under-investigation-after-allegations-of-inappropriate-behavior/| access-date= 2026-09-04| website= New Jersey Globe| language= en-US}}</ref>

==== Public perception ====
On April 9, 2026, the [[Eagleton Institute of Politics]] released a poll showing Sherrill's approval at 45%, while 29% disapproved.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-04-09 |title=New Jerseyans Give Sherrill Solid Ratings at 100 Days, but Uncertainty Remains |url=https://www.rutgers.edu/news/new-jerseyans-give-sherrill-solid-ratings-100-days-uncertainty-remains |access-date=2026-04-13 |website=www.rutgers.edu |language=en}}</ref> On March 31, a [[Fairleigh Dickinson University]] poll showed Sherrill at 58% approval and 34% disapproval, giving her a net approval of +24,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schipper |first=Dina |date=2026-03-31 |title=FDU Poll Finds Strong Approval Numbers at Start of Sherrill's Term |url=https://www.fdu.edu/news/fdu-poll-finds-strong-approval-numbers-at-start-of-sherrills-term/ |access-date=2026-04-13 |website=Fairleigh Dickinson University |language=en-US}}</ref> notably contrasting with her fellow freshman governor [[Abigail Spanberger]]'s net approval of +1 in a [[The Washington Post|Washington Post]] poll.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Yancey |first=Dwayne |date=2026-04-08 |title=Poll shows Spanberger's approval rating starts out lower than the last eight governors. Here's why. |url=https://cardinalnews.org/2026/04/08/poll-shows-spanbergers-approval-rating-starts-out-lower-than-the-last-eight-governors-heres-why/ |access-date=2026-04-13 |website=Cardinal News |language=en-US}}</ref>

